// Initialization-order precondition boundary (reordered per-actor initialization).
// The two-actor payload fragment is declared exactly as in the positive sibling:
// the same Sender and Receiver classes, the same known rebec binding receiver0, and
// the same message-server surface. The ONLY difference is that main instantiates the
// actors in the order [receiver0, sender0] rather than the declared topology order
// [sender0, receiver0].
//
// This is well-formed Timed Rebeca and RMC accepts it (main-order carries no runtime
// meaning in Rebeca; the rebecs form a concurrent set). The verified ReLico startup
// construction, however, aligns per-actor initial stores POSITIONALLY: its
// alignStartupEntries requires the initial-store domain to equal the actor domain in
// declared order. A reordered initialization therefore falls outside the translation's
// proven-correct input domain, and the family parser refuses it rather than emitting an
// unverified translation. See Relico/Tests/GlobalMultiStorePayloadInitialization.lean:
// reordered_initial_stores_rejected and reordered_alignment_is_none.
reactiveclass Sender(100) {
    knownrebecs{
        Receiver receiver0;
    }

    Sender() {
        self.sendMessage(1);
        self.keepAlive() after(1);
    }

    @priority(1)
    msgsrv sendMessage(int data){
        receiver0.receiveMessage(data);
    }

    msgsrv keepAlive() {
        self.keepAlive() after(1);
    }
}

reactiveclass Receiver(100) {
    @priority(1)
    msgsrv receiveMessage(int data){
        // Process data
    }
}

main {
    Receiver receiver0():();
    Sender sender0(receiver0):();
}
